Goal requires origin_availability_locked and origin_availability_confirmed both true. lock_origin_availability gives both. However lock_origin_availability also requires origin_node_ready (not occupied). If we lock before loading payload, origin_node_occupied will be false. After locking, we can load payload (which requires not origin_availability_locked? Actually load_origin_payload precondition includes not origin_availability_locked ?origin_availability. So we cannot load after locking because load requires not locked. So we need to load before locking? Let's examine load_origin_payload precondition: candidate_prepared ?origin_availability, origin_availability_node_linked ?origin_availability ?origin_node, cargo_available ?payload_unit, not origin_availability_locked ?origin_availability. So loading requires origin_availability NOT locked. After loading, effect sets origin_availability_locked true. So we need to load before locking. However goal requires origin_availability_locked true and origin_availability_confirmed true. Loading sets origin_availability_locked true but does not set origin_availability_confirmed. So after loading, we have locked but not confirmed. We need confirmed true. We can get confirmed via lock_origin_availability? But lock requires not origin_availability_locked (precondition). So cannot lock after loading because locked already true. Could we get confirmed via unload_origin_payload? Unload sets origin_availability_confirmed true, but also requires not origin_availability_confirmed? Actually precondition: not origin_availability_confirmed ?origin_availability. After loading, origin_availability_confirmed is false (since not set). So we can unload after loading to get confirmed true, but unload also requires origin_node_occupied and origin_payload_linked (which we have after load). Unload effect: origin_node_ready, origin_availability_confirmed, cargo_available, not origin_payload_linked. It does NOT affect origin_availability_locked (remains locked). So after load then unload, we have origin_availability_locked true (from load), origin_availability_confirmed true (from unload), origin_node_occupied true (load set, unload does not remove), origin_node_ready true (from unload), cargo_available true (from unload). Good! So we can achieve origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready, origin_availability_locked and confirmed, and cargo available again for destination load.

Thus sequence for origin: load_origin_payload then unload_origin_payload.

Now for destination: we need destination_node_occupied and destination_node_ready, destination_availability_locked and confirmed, and cargo_available for load (we will have after origin unload). Steps: activate_destination_node (makes destination_node_ready true, requires not destination_node_occupied and not destination_node_ready). Then load_destination_payload (requires candidate_prepared ?destination_availability, destination_availability_node_linked ?destination_availability ?destination_node, cargo_available ?payload_unit, not destination_availability_locked ?destination_availability). Effect: destination_node_occupied true, destination_availability_locked true, destination_payload_linked true, cargo_available false. After load, we have destination_node_occupied true, destination_node_ready still true (from activation), destination_availability_locked true, destination_availability_confirmed? Not set. Need destination_availability_confirmed true. We can get that via unload_destination_payload? But we need destination_node_occupied to stay true; unload would remove destination_payload_linked and set destination_node_ready true (already true) and destination_availability_confirmed true, cargo_available true, but would NOT affect destination_node_occupied? Let's check unload_destination_payload effect: (destination_node_ready ?destination_node) (destination_availability_confirmed ?destination_availability) (cargo_available ?payload_unit) (not (destination_payload_linked ?destination_availability ?payload_unit)). It does not affect destination_node_occupied or destination_availability_locked. So after load then unload, we would have destination_node_occupied true (from load, not removed), destination_node_ready true (from activation and unload), destination_availability_locked true (from load, not removed), destination_availability_confirmed true (from unload), cargo_available true (from unload). Good! So we can load then unload destination to get confirmed true while keeping occupied and locked.

Thus we need to load and unload both origin and destination payloads, leaving nodes occupied and ready, availabilities locked and confirmed, cargo available again (though not needed after). Good.

Now we need to assemble transport leg. Which assemble action? We need transport_leg_assembled, transport_leg_origin_linked, transport_leg_destination_linked, transport_leg_loading_authorized. Not require leg loaded. So we can use assemble_transport_leg (basic) which requires origin_availability_locked, destination_availability_locked, origin_node_ready, destination_node_ready, origin_availability_confirmed, destination_availability_confirmed, transport_leg_available, origin_availability_node_linked, destination_availability_node_linked. After we have done load/unload for both origin and destination, we will have origin_availability_locked true, origin_availability_confirmed true, origin_node_ready true (from unload), origin_node_occupied true (from load). Similarly destination: locked, confirmed, ready, occupied. Also we have origin_availability_node_linked and destination_availability_node_linked already true. Transport_leg_available initially true. So we can call assemble_transport_leg after we have those conditions.

After assemble, we need transport_leg_loading_authorized true. There's action authorize_transport_leg_loading with parameters ?transport_leg ?origin_availability ?route_task_profile. Precondition: transport_leg_assembled ?transport_leg, origin_availability_locked ?origin_availability, candidate_task_profile_linked ?origin_availability ?route_task_profile, not transport_leg_loading_authorized ?transport_leg. Effect: transport_leg_loading_authorized ?transport_leg.

We have origin_availability_locked true, candidate_task_profile_linked origin_availability_alpha route_task_profile_alpha true (we attached earlier). transport_leg_assembled will be true after assemble. So we can authorize.

Now we need dispatch_ready for origin and destination. Use mark_origin_availability_dispatch_ready and mark_destination_availability_dispatch_ready. Preconditions: origin_availability_locked, origin_availability_confirmed, transport_leg_assembled, transport_leg_loading_authorized, not dispatch_ready. So after we have those, we can mark dispatch_ready.

We also need dispatch_allocated for origin and destination. Currently we have dispatch_allocated shift_candidate_alpha true, but not for origin or destination. We need to allocate dispatch slot to origin_availability_alpha and destination_availability_alpha. Action allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate: parameters ?candidate - candidate_entity ?dispatch_slot - dispatch_slot ?route_task_profile - route_task_profile. Precondition: dispatch_ready ?candidate, candidate_task_profile_linked ?candidate ?route_task_profile, dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot, not dispatch_allocated ?candidate. Effect: dispatch_allocated ?candidate, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate ?dispatch_slot, not dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot.

Thus we need dispatch_ready for origin and destination, candidate_task_profile_linked for each to route_task_profile_alpha (we have for origin after attach, destination already has link), dispatch_slot_available (initially true). After allocating to origin, dispatch_slot_available becomes false. Then we need to allocate to destination, but dispatch_slot_available will be false after first allocation. We need another dispatch slot? We only have dispatch_slot_alpha. Goal requires both origin and destination to have candidate_dispatch_slot_linked to dispatch_slot_alpha. That would require both to be linked to same dispatch slot, but dispatch_slot_available can only be true for one at a time (since allocating consumes availability). However the allocate action removes dispatch_slot_available. So we cannot allocate same slot to two candidates simultaneously. However goal requires both candidate_dispatch_slot_linked to same dispatch_slot_alpha. Is that possible? Let's examine the predicate: candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate - candidate_entity ?dispatch_slot - dispatch_slot. There's no constraint that a dispatch slot can be linked to only one candidate. The allocate action removes dispatch_slot_available, but does not prevent linking another candidate to same slot? The effect includes candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate ?dispatch_slot and not dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot. If we try to allocate again, precondition requires dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot, which would be false after first allocation. So cannot allocate second candidate to same slot while slot is not available. However maybe we can allocate to origin, then later deallocate? There's no release action for dispatch slot. But maybe we can allocate to origin, then later allocate to destination after making slot available again via some action? There's no release action. However the dispatch action execute_*_dispatch has effect (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). So after we dispatch a candidate, the dispatch slot becomes available again. Indeed execute_origin_dispatch effect includes (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). So we could allocate dispatch slot to origin, then dispatch origin (which will make slot available again), then allocate to destination, then dispatch destination. Goal requires dispatch_allocated for both origin and destination (still true after dispatch? The allocate action adds dispatch_allocated and does not remove it. Dispatch action does not remove dispatch_allocated. So after dispatch, dispatch_allocated remains true. Good. Also goal requires dispatched origin_availability_alpha (we will dispatch origin). It does not require dispatched destination_availability_alpha? Actually goal includes (dispatched origin_availability_alpha) only, not destination. So we need to dispatch origin but not necessarily destination. However goal also requires dispatch_allocated destination_availability_alpha and dispatch_ready destination_availability_alpha. So destination must be dispatch_allocated and dispatch_ready but not necessarily dispatched. That's okay: we can allocate dispatch slot to destination, but not dispatch it. However after allocating to destination, dispatch_slot_available becomes false, and we cannot allocate to origin later unless we dispatch something to free slot. But we need origin also allocated. So we need to allocate to origin first, dispatch origin (freeing slot), then allocate to destination (slot becomes unavailable again). After that, origin remains allocated (dispatch_allocated true) and dispatched true, destination allocated but not dispatched. Goal does not require destination dispatched, only dispatch_allocated and dispatch_ready. So that works.

Thus sequence:

- Allocate dispatch slot to origin_availability_alpha (requires dispatch_ready origin). After allocation, dispatch_slot_available false.
- Execute origin dispatch (execute_origin_dispatch) -> makes vehicle_available vehicle_unit_alpha, dispatch_slot_available true, dispatched origin true.
- After dispatch, dispatch_slot_available true again.
- Allocate dispatch slot to destination_availability_alpha (requires dispatch_ready destination). After allocation, dispatch_slot_available false.
- Do not dispatch destination (keep allocated but not dispatched). Goal requires dispatch_allocated destination_availability_alpha (true) and dispatch_ready destination_availability_alpha (true). It does not require dispatched destination. So fine.

Now we need dispatch_ready for origin and destination before allocation.

Thus steps:

1. Prepare origin: detach task profile from destination, attach to origin, confirm prepared.
2. Activate origin node.
3. Load origin payload.
4. Unload origin payload (to free cargo, keep occupied, get confirmed).
5. Activate destination node.
6. Load destination payload.
7. Unload destination payload (to get confirmed, keep occupied, lock).
   At this point we have origin and destination locked, confirmed, nodes ready and occupied, cargo available again.
8. Assemble transport leg (requires origin/destination locked, confirmed, nodes ready, node linked, transport leg available).
9. Authorize transport leg loading (requires origin_availability_locked, candidate_task_profile_linked origin to route_task_profile, transport_leg_assembled).
10. Mark origin dispatch ready (requires origin_availability_locked, origin_availability_confirmed, transport_leg_assembled, transport_leg_loading_authorized, not dispatch_ready).
11. Mark destination dispatch ready (similar).
12. Allocate dispatch slot to origin (requires dispatch_ready origin, candidate_task_profile_linked origin to route_task_profile, dispatch_slot_available).
13. Execute origin dispatch (requires dispatch_allocated origin, candidate_vehicle_linked origin vehicle_unit_alpha, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked origin dispatch_slot_alpha, not dispatched origin). After this, vehicle_available vehicle_unit_alpha true, dispatch_slot_available true, dispatched origin true.
14. Allocate dispatch slot to destination (requires dispatch_ready destination, candidate_task_profile_linked destination to route_task_profile, dispatch_slot_available).
15. (Optionally) we could also dispatch destination but not needed; goal does not require dispatched destination. However goal requires dispatch_allocated destination_availability_alpha (we have after allocation) and dispatch_ready destination_availability_alpha (we have). It does not require dispatched destination. So we stop.

Now we need to ensure we have candidate_task_profile_linked for destination still true after we detached earlier? We detached from destination to make route_task_profile available, then attached to origin. Destination still has link because we didn't detach it. So candidate_task_profile_linked destination_availability_alpha route_task_profile_alpha remains true. Good.

